WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.330
. Today, we're going to be speaking with Mark
Tushnet, who is emeritus professor at Harvard

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:10.780
Law School and also a specialist in U.S. and
comparative constitutional law.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:11.780
So great to have you on.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.220
I really appreciate it.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.000
Thanks for having me.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.670
So I guess to get into this conversation,
there's been significant debate over the last

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:24.999
few months since the passing of Supreme Court
Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg about the correct

00:00:24.999 --> 00:00:30.499
number of Supreme Court justices and questions
like whether there should, for example, be

00:00:30.499 --> 00:00:33.040
a term limits for Supreme Court justices.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.430
But there's another approach, and that's one
of questioning whether we really even need

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.070
or should have a Supreme Court.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.890
So, I mean, let's let's just start there.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:47.290
What do you believe the purpose of the Supreme
Court was originally?

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.110
And does it still fulfill that purpose?

00:00:50.110 --> 00:01:00.440
Well, the basic idea of the court was that
you needed somebody that would be able to

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:08.579
enforce the provisions of the Constitution,
you could expect politicians to debate the

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:13.090
constitutional issues and reach compromises
and so on.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:20.850
And so politics might be one way of making
sure that people adhere to the Constitution.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:30.610
But early on, people started worrying that
politicians might find it in their mutual

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:40.969
interest to do something that was inconsistent
with the Constitution, to agree on some program

00:01:40.969 --> 00:01:43.659
that gave the president too much power.

00:01:43.659 --> 00:01:44.659
Sure.

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:52.299
Or more likely, that we have what we think
of today, enact programs that violated individual

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:53.560
rights.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:02:02.090
And the thought was you could insert a court
into the process so as to to check against

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.249
those possibilities.

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:11.500
And at this point in time, is that number
one still necessary?

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.709
And number two is that the primary activity
that the Supreme Court is engaged in checking

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:17.909
that.

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:20.100
Well, the second question.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:26.180
No, actually, it turns out not the primary
thing the court does, things that don't get

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:34.599
as much attention is interpret statutes, tell
Congress, passes a statute and it's got some

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:38.560
provisions in it that aren't clear.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:47.160
And the Supreme Court comes up with an authoritative
interpretation of the statute on the first.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:57.489
I think it's it's a tricky question about
whether the court is now either meeting or

00:02:57.489 --> 00:03:04.390
doing too much in terms of enforcing constitutional
provisions.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:12.110
The reason it's tricky is that we now understand
that there's a lot of that would put it this

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:18.150
way, reasonable disagreement about what the
Constitution actually means.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:28.959
And if everybody thinks they know the Constitution
prohibits things that they don't like happening.

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:41.180
But it turns out that it's very difficult
to say that very often legislatures or the

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:49.720
president actually do things that nobody could
fairly say are consistent with the Constitution.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.680
Occasionally they did once in a while.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:58.970
But most of the time what the court is doing
is saying you have this interpretation of

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.890
the Constitution.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:04.150
Maybe it's a reasonable one, but we have a
different one.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:12.860
And we're going to stop you from doing what
you think is consistent with the Constitution.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.349
So in terms of.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:21.280
Yeah, so in terms in terms of if there were
no Supreme Court, is it is it as simple as

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.830
the the next lowest court's decision?

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.190
It becomes the final decision.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:31.039
Is it that simple or is there more rearranging
that would need to be done?

00:04:31.039 --> 00:04:33.800
Well, you'd probably want to do more rearranging.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:40.800
I mean, what if it were if you just took the
court out of the process entirely, then you'd

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:47.150
end up with the possibility of a court in
California saying something constitutionally,

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:48.150
OK?

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:52.009
And a court in Massachusetts saying it's not
OK.

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:53.640
Yeah, that's not impossible.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.860
I mean, you can live with a world like that,
but it's sort of awkward.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.150
We think it's our Constitution.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:07.050
So you probably if you take the court out
of the process, you probably want to take

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:09.310
the lower courts out of it as well.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:10.310
And how would you do that?

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.660
Would you establish some kind of a.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.199
Well, I mean, I won't I won't even pretend
to know how how you would do it, how how would

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:17.199
it be done?

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:20.050
What would what would be the way that you
would rearrange that process?

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:27.240
Well, my preferred solution would be to say,
yes, we can still keep the courts involved

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.280
for reasons I'll get to in just a moment.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.259
But they should apply a standard that we could
even write it into the Constitution, that

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:44.389
they should hold things unconstitutional only
if they are my phrase is manifestly unreasonable.

00:05:44.389 --> 00:05:54.889
And that sharply reduces the degree to which
the courts, any court could could intervene

00:05:54.889 --> 00:06:00.090
in constitutional interpretation, would still
give them some role, but only for extreme

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:06.789
cases in terms of the more common reforms
that are sometimes mentioned or changes to

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:16.199
the court, things like changes to the process
by which by the mere chance or timing of who

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:20.620
is president at the time that a vacancy is
created, changing that.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:25.740
So it's not just the whim of whether there's
a Republican or a Democrat in the White House

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:31.500
changing the number of justices on the court,
putting term limits for being on the court.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.220
Do you like any of these?

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:34.220
All of them?

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.500
None of them.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:43.240
So I'm in favor of any program that's likely
to have some effect on reducing the role of

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.450
the court in our political life.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:52.160
OK, people, the most popular thing is the
things you've mentioned are term limits.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.230
And I'm.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:07:02.129
I've signed on to a term limits proposal,
people who favor them to think that if you

00:07:02.129 --> 00:07:12.650
have sort of a regular process of arrival
and departure, then the stakes for each appointment

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:21.810
will go down and that might lower the willingness
of the court to get involved in deep political

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:22.980
matters.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:26.280
The appointment process would be more routine,
less highly politicized.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:32.139
I think that might happen.

00:07:32.139 --> 00:07:39.220
The expanding the size of the court, which
is also something I currently favor, would

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:47.360
temporarily actually make the court more prominent
because people would focus on on.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.389
Well, they've just.

00:07:50.389 --> 00:07:56.240
Expanded the court to change the rulings that
are coming out of it, but people also seem

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:05.380
to think that eventually there will be some
sort of equilibrium where first Democrats

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:08.409
expand the court and Republicans expand the
court.

00:08:08.409 --> 00:08:14.090
And eventually people come to understand that
that's not a terrible, terribly great way

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:15.919
of doing things.

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:23.330
And what you really want to do is, again,
lower the temperature of the court's activity

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:28.639
so that people talk about reducing the legitimacy
of the court.

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:31.569
I think that's basically a good thing.

00:08:31.569 --> 00:08:36.760
And so if court expansion had that effect,
that would be OK with me, too.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:43.530
When it comes to the idea of and I know that
this is sort of a different, different question,

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:49.730
the idea of constitutional originalism, which
is a very, very big debate to me.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:55.620
There's a few elements that that sort of don't
pass the sniff test that are very often mentioned.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:57.490
And I'm curious to get your thoughts on it.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:02.410
One is, if we're talking about constitutional
originalism as thought at the time of the

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:07.930
Constitution's creation, when we talk about
bearing arms, we would have to account for

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:13.149
the types of arms that existed at the time
and recognize a lot of what's available today

00:09:13.149 --> 00:09:17.800
would be out of scope, even even theoretically
imaginable at the time.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:23.210
When you talk about cruel and unusual punishment,
what we have today is dramatically different

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:26.560
than what would have fallen under that definition
at the time.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:32.940
So how what is your sense in terms of this
debate around originalism, which is often

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:38.230
put forward by those who seem unwilling to
concede some of these points on cruel and

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:41.020
unusual and on the Second Amendment that I've
brought up?

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:48.510
But I think we have to distinguish between
two kinds of originalism.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:54.440
There's the originalism that academics have
come up with, OK?

00:09:54.440 --> 00:10:01.930
And academics have a decent account of academic
originalists have have come up with a decent

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:10.760
account of the Second Amendment problems and
how we can deal with them and the cruel and

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:15.760
unusual punishment provision and a whole bunch
of other things.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.570
Equality for women is a significant matter.

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:27.149
So the academic theory actually has a certain
amount of credibility.

00:10:27.149 --> 00:10:33.760
But then there's the practice of originalism
by judges who say they are originalists.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:38.339
And that's sharply different from what the
academics say.

00:10:38.339 --> 00:10:41.040
And it's entirely inconsistent.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:48.790
They are originalist when the results they
come up with fit, they're mostly conservative

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:57.779
ideology and then they depart from originalism
when they haven't figured out how to.

00:10:57.779 --> 00:11:02.860
Defend their conservative positions in originalist
terms.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:08.220
The best example of that, incidentally, is
affirmative action.

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:18.360
There's just no no credible should the academics
have a credible way of defending or attacking

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:22.750
affirmative action on originalist grounds.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:27.540
But conservative judges haven't even bothered
to try.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:32.840
They just think that it's inconsistent with
some fundamental principle of equality that

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:38.230
they're committed to without trying to link
that principle to original understandings.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:43.550
OK, this is good because it adds a level of
nuance when we hear those who are not particularly

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:49.529
well-educated on this bring up this issue
of of original intent in order to defend some

00:11:49.529 --> 00:11:51.440
of their conservative ideals.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:58.120
So when we talk about constitutional originalism,
you're pointing out there is judicial constitutional

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:04.459
originalism and then there is academic constitutional
originalism and that these are two different

00:12:04.459 --> 00:12:05.459
things.

00:12:05.459 --> 00:12:10.330
And as you're putting forward the idea that
academic constitutional originalism does deal

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:14.610
with some of these apparent contradictions,
maybe we could dig into one of them, like,

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:20.470
for example, on 2nd Amendment and the idea
of what arms referred to hundreds of years

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.550
ago versus the weapons available today.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:29.470
How would the academic originalists sort of
deal with that?

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:36.089
The academic account is actually there are
two versions of the academic account.

00:12:36.089 --> 00:12:38.920
One is.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:47.470
Tied to what we mostly think about these days,
and it is you say.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:54.790
You have to think about the kinds of weapons
that people had in mind that were understood

00:12:54.790 --> 00:13:00.310
to be covered by the Second Amendment right
and.

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:05.980
When they thought about and talked about the
weapons that were covered by the Second Amendment,

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:13.080
they referred to weapons that everybody has
access to or in the current phrase, weapons

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.250
in common use.

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:22.010
And so now you translate that to the current
circumstances and you ask what weapons are

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:24.889
used today.

00:13:24.889 --> 00:13:28.149
So weapons and current use then were muskets
and things like that.

00:13:28.149 --> 00:13:33.190
They're not in current use now, but ordinary
handguns are in use.

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:37.460
On the other hand, bazookas are not encouraged.

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:38.670
So you're making an analogy.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:43.430
In other words, you have to you have to think
of what was what would be analogous to those

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:44.920
weapons today.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:47.500
Yeah, OK, exactly.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:49.910
So that's one way of doing it.

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:51.529
The other way is much more radical.

00:13:51.529 --> 00:13:58.810
And actually, I think in my own view, is more
connected to what was actually going on.

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:03.060
The original understanding of the second view
of the Second Amendment was that it was a

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:15.810
way of ensuring that ordinary people would
be able to fight against an overreaching government.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:22.269
They would be able to arm themselves to protect
themselves, not against criminals, but against

00:14:22.269 --> 00:14:24.579
soldiers.

00:14:24.579 --> 00:14:30.920
And given what the military has today, that
would imply a very, very high a standard for

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:31.920
what it would mean.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:32.920
Exactly.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:40.490
And no no contemporary defender of the Second
Amendment except people associated with these

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:45.100
Michigan militia types who want to go there.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:51.440
And so the judges say, well, we don't you
know, they may have had this original understanding

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:57.019
about what was called the insurrectionist
view, but but that's not what we think the

00:14:57.019 --> 00:14:59.750
original understanding was.

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:10.370
And they do that precisely for this sort of
instrumental reason and focused reasons.

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:15.670
They don't want to say people have a right
to have bazookas or anti-tank weapons.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:19.730
Right now, it seems like you could apply a
similar standard to this idea of cruel and

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:20.730
unusual punishments.

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:21.730
Right.

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:25.209
Where where you would say, well, just because
there are things available now that weren't

00:15:25.209 --> 00:15:29.430
available then and some of the things that
were done then have gone out of style, we

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:32.810
have to sort of evaluate what is analogous
at this time.

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:34.279
Right, right.

00:15:34.279 --> 00:15:39.680
And and here, in fact, again, the academic
position is.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:41.459
Yeah, that's right.

00:15:41.459 --> 00:15:50.829
And it might be that in particular, capital
punishment or life in prison without parole

00:15:50.829 --> 00:15:51.850
for juveniles.

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:58.930
Those might be inconsistent with the fundamental
ideas that were understood to be written into

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:03.180
the cruel and unusual punishment punishments
clause.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:10.430
And again, what judicial originalists do is
they abandon the originalism and they say,

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:15.050
well, look, the text says capital crimes.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:21.639
So whatever they thought, cruel and unusual
punishment meant, they also thought capital

00:16:21.639 --> 00:16:26.440
punishment was constitutionally permissible.

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:32.170
And so, again, you see this inconsistency
between the practice of conservative judges

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:40.850
who want to uphold capital punishment and
originalism as it might be coherently worked

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:41.850
out.

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:43.589
Absolutely fascinating.

00:16:43.589 --> 00:16:49.910
I think the big takeaway is, no matter what
you call your philosophy, there is still debate

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:56.880
to be had in figuring out exactly how we define
it and different versions of it.

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:02.889
That's right, I mean, I think the the again,
I'm an academic, the academic view of these

00:17:02.889 --> 00:17:12.690
debates is that with relatively minor exceptions.

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:19.860
Whatever your theoretical position is, you're
going to be able to fit your current political

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:28.190
views into it, and that means that the theoretical
stuff really, really isn't doing any serious

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:29.540
work.

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:34.980
What's going on is you're a liberal or conservative
and that's how you rule.

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:38.860
Yes, very well described by by behavioral
economists.

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:45.280
In fact, like Daniel Kahneman and and others
who have talked a lot about that type of motivated

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:50.170
reasoning, which clearly exists within the
issue of constitutional scholarship as well.

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:56.150
We've been speaking with Mark Tushnet, emeritus
professor at Harvard Law School, a professor.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:57.660
It's been such a pleasure speaking with you.

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:00.280
I so appreciate your time.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:02.610
I enjoyed it and appreciate being on The David
Pakman Show at David Pakman Dotcom.

